# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your keys.
# `.env` is gitignored — never commit real keys.

# One model provider is required; configure either or both. With both set,
# LLM_PROVIDER picks the *primary* — and the other one is then used as the assist
# model, which is worth having: see the ASSIST_* block further down. With one
# provider set, it is used automatically and carries every agent.
#
#   google      Gemini via Google AI Studio. 500 requests/day on the default
#               gemini-3.5-flash-lite — enough to iterate. Use this by default.
#   openrouter  Free open-weight models, nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b by default.
#               Stronger, but 50 requests/day total (~3 mapping runs), so it is
#               the wrong home for the agents that make most of the requests.
# LLM_PROVIDER=google

# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — no card required.
GEMINI_API_KEY=AIza...

# Get one at https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...

# Required by the GitHub tools and the test suite.
# Create at https://github.com/settings/tokens — read-only access to public
# repositories is enough. Without a token the GitHub API allows only 60
# requests/hour (5000 with one) and rejects code search outright.
GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_...

# Optional per-provider model overrides. See repo_cartographer/models.py for the
# candidates worth trying and what each one trades away.
#
# Careful with Gemini: the capable Flash models allow only 20 requests/day each —
# one mapping run — while the Flash-Lite models allow 500.
# GOOGLE_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The second model, and the second budget that comes with it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The limit that stops a run is requests per *minute*, and those limits are
# published per model — Google's usage dashboard reports one row per model name.
# So a second model is a second budget, and moving one agent onto it is capacity
# gained without a single request saved.
#
# With keys for both providers set, this happens on its own: the doc-writer runs
# on the other provider's default model and everything else stays on the primary.
# Nothing below needs setting for that.
#
# Which agent moves is not arbitrary. The doc-writer is the only one whose
# failure mode this system already guards twice — Phase 4 took away its
# repository access entirely, and Phase 6 checks every path it cites with no
# model involved — so a weaker model there writes worse sentences but cannot
# invent a file. The orchestrator has to emit parallel tool calls, write a brief
# `briefing.py` can parse, and relay the guide verbatim; weaker models are bad at
# all three and fail silently at each. The explorer decides what to read and
# writes the notes everything else rests on, and it spends more requests than the
# other two together.
#
# ASSIST_MODEL  overrides which model that is. The provider is inferred from the
#               name — a slash means OpenRouter — and its key must be set.
#               Two models from one provider is a valid answer: they are still
#               two budgets.
# ASSIST_ROLES  overrides which agents move. Comma-separated, from
#               `orchestrator`, `explorer`, `doc-writer`. Empty puts every agent
#               back on one model. A name that is not a role is refused rather
#               than ignored, because ignoring it would look like it worked.
#
# Read the trade before widening this. Free OpenRouter models are capable but not
# uniformly so, and the two agents left on the primary are there because their
# regressions are the ones nothing in this system would catch. `run_evals.py` is
# what tells you whether a move cost you anything; an answer that still reads
# well is not evidence.
#
# Not `openrouter/free`, either — it is a real id that picks a free model at
# random per request, so no two runs are comparable. That breaks Phase 2's
# definition of done and every eval score, both of which compare runs.
# ASSIST_MODEL=inclusionai/ling-3.0-flash:free
# ASSIST_ROLES=doc-writer

# Optional tracing. Phase 4's definition of done is a trace showing each
# sub-agent in its own context window, and this is what turns it on: with these
# set, every run appears in LangSmith with the orchestrator, explorer and
# doc-writer as separate nested threads you can click into.
#
# Tracing is provider-independent — it records what the graph did, not who served
# the model — so it works the same on Gemini or OpenRouter and costs nothing
# against either quota. Free key at https://smith.langchain.com/settings.
#
# `scripts/show_contexts.py` reports the same split locally as numbers, and needs
# none of this. Set these when you want to read a transcript, not a total.
# LANGSMITH_TRACING=true
# LANGSMITH_API_KEY=lsv2_pt_...
# LANGSMITH_PROJECT=repo-cartographer

# Requests per minute to allow *per model*. Agents sharing a model share one
# budget, because the provider counts their requests together; agents on
# different models get their own, because the published limits are per model.
# Phase 4b dispatches explorers concurrently, and three agents in their own tool
# loops reach a free-tier ceiling in seconds; models.py rate limits to 80% of the
# tier's published limit so they queue instead of 429ing. Raise this on a paid
# tier — it is the one number that decides whether fan-out survives.
# REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=15

# How many times the client may re-send a request the provider rejected. Two,
# because the default in langchain-google-genai is six and every one of those six
# is a real request the provider counts — against the per-minute limit that
# caused the rejection, and against the per-day limit that had nothing to do with
# it. That is how one bad minute takes a chunk out of a whole day, and it is why a
# dashboard can read 17 requests in a minute against a limiter set to 12.
# MODEL_MAX_RETRIES=2

# The one irreversible capability, and it is off unless you turn it on.
# `open_pull_request` writes a branch, a file and a draft pull request into a
# repository — visible to its maintainers, and not undone by running anything
# again. Two independent guards stand in front of it:
#
#   1. `interrupt_on` pauses the run and waits for a human decision. That stops
#      the *agent* acting alone, and it assumes someone is there to answer.
#   2. This flag stops the *deployment* acting at all. It survives an unattended
#      run, a script someone runs to see what happens, and a resume that
#      auto-approves — none of which guard 1 covers.
#
# Leave it unset unless you mean it, and point it at a repository you control.
# `scripts/prove_approval_gate.py` demonstrates the whole gate with this off,
# and refuses to run with it on.
# ALLOW_PULL_REQUESTS=true
